#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091925 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4149b2fe-4243-595e-af7f-851b308f7eb1" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/id #uuid "c690991e-6029-5067-b97a-67c5e62b7f47" :question/math-academy-id "e-10109" :question/problem "A store has $40$ employees, of which $21$ are female. The store is divided into three departments, $A, B$, and $C$. There are $7$ female workers in department $A$, while there are $8$ female workers in department $C$. What is the probability that a randomly selected store worker is a female that works in department $B$?" :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that the chosen worker is a female, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected worker works department $A$,\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected worker works department $B$,\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected worker works department $C$.\n\nThen the disjoint union $A \\sqcup B \\sqcup C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nRecall that the law of total probability states that\n\n$$\nP(F) = P(F \\cap A) + P(F \\cap B) + P(F \\cap C)\n$$\n\nWe wish to find $P(F \\cap B)$:\n\n- To calculate $P(F \\cap A)$, we take the number of females who work in department $A$ $(7)$ and divide by the total number of workers $(40)$. So, we have\n$P(F \\cap A) = \\dfrac{7}{40}$.\n- To calculate $P(F \\cap C)$, we take the number of females who work in department $C$ $(8)$ and divide by the total number of workers $(40)$. So, we have\n$P(F \\cap C) = \\dfrac{8}{40}$.\n- Also, we're given that\n$P(F) = \\dfrac{21}{40}$.\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(F \\cap B)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(F) &= P(F∩A) + P(F∩B) + P(F∩C) \\\\\n\\frac{21}{40} &= \\frac{7}{40} + P(F∩B) + \\frac{8}{40} \\\\\nP(F∩B) &= \\frac{21}{40} - \\frac{7}{40} - \\frac{8}{40}. \\\\\nP(F∩B) &= \\frac{6}{40} \\\\\nP(F∩B) &= \\frac{3}{20}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091926 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96f54294-8bb4-5338-969b-76c8d1536daa" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Law of Total Probability in Terms of Conditional Probability"}] :question/id #uuid "1104f073-7f90-51ce-9e95-aae0935ca852" :question/math-academy-id "e-10110" :question/problem "Three factories produce the same spare part for a particular car model. Factory ${A}$ produces $32\\%$ of the overall parts, of which $1.5\\%$ are defective. Factory ${B}$ produces $33\\%$ of the overall parts, of which $2\\%$ are defective, and Factory ${C}$ produces $35\\%$ of the overall parts, of which $1.4\\%$ are defective. If one car part is selected randomly, what is the probability that it is defective?" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly selected part is defective, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected part was produced by Factory ${A}$,\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected part was produced by Factory ${B}$,\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected part was produced by Factory ${C}$.\n\nThen the disjoint union $A \\sqcup B \\sqcup C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected part is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(D) &= P(D∩A) + P(D∩B) + P(D∩C) \\\\\n&= P(D \\mid A)P(A) + P(D \\mid B)P(B) + P(D \\mid C)P(C)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since Factory ${A}$ produces $32\\%$ of the parts, Factory ${B}$ produces $33 \\%$ of the parts, and Factory ${C}$ produces $35 \\%$ of the parts, we have\n$P(A) = 0.32, \\qquad P(B) = 0.33, \\qquad P(C)=0.35$.\n- Since $1.5\\%$ of the parts produced by Factory ${A}$, $2\\%$ of the parts produced by Factory ${B}$, and $1.4\\%$ of the parts produced by Factory ${C}$ are defective, we have\n$P(D \\mid A) = 0.015, \\qquad P(D \\mid B) = 0.02, \\qquad P(D \\mid C) = 0.014$.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected part is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(D) &= P(D \\mid A)P(A) + P(D \\mid B)P(B) + P(D \\mid C)P(C) \\\\\n&= (0.015)(0.32) + (0.02)(0.33) + (0.014)(0.35) \\\\\n&= 0.0048 + 0.0066 + 0.0049 \\\\\n&= 0.0163\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091927 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0ebe700-63af-57d4-ae8f-9e09dac5cf76" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Conditional Probability Using the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/id #uuid "ae39bb45-ac10-562a-bac6-4f192c5f0aed" :question/math-academy-id "e-10111" :question/problem "Three baskets, $A, B$, and $C$, contain apples. It is known that $\\dfrac25$ of the apples in basket $A$ and $\\dfrac14$ of the apples in basket $B$ are red. A basket is selected at random, and an apple is randomly chosen from the basket. If the probability that the selected apple is red is $\\dfrac13$, what proportion of the apples from basket $C$ are red?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the event that the selected apple is red, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected apple is from basket ${A}$,\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected apple is from basket ${B}$,\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected apple is from basket ${C}$.\n\nThen the disjoint union $A \\sqcup B \\sqcup C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a red apple, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(R) &= P(R∩A) + P(R∩B) + P(R∩C) \\\\\n&= P(R \\mid A)P(A) + P(R \\mid B)P(B) + P(R \\mid C)P(C)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since a basket is randomly selected and there are $3$ baskets, we have\n$P(A) = P(B) = P(C) = \\dfrac{1}{3}$.\n- Since $\\dfrac25$ of the apples in basket $A$ and $\\dfrac14$ of the apples in basket $B$ are red, we have\n$P(R \\mid A)= \\dfrac25, \\qquad P(R \\mid B) = \\dfrac14$.\n- Since the probability of picking a red apple from any of the three baskets is $\\dfrac13$, we have\n$P(R) = \\dfrac13$.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(R \\mid C)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(R) &= P(R\\begin{vmatrix}A)P(A) + P(R \\mid B)P(B) + P(R \\mid C)P(C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}|= (\\frac{2}{5})(\\frac{1}{3}) + (\\frac{1}{4})(\\frac{1}{3}) + P(R \\mid C)(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}|= \\frac{2}{15} + \\frac{1}{12} + \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot P(R \\mid C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot P(R \\mid C)|= \\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{2}{15} - \\frac{1}{12} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot P(R \\mid C)|= \\frac{7}{60} \\\\ P(R\\end{vmatrix}C)|= \\frac{7}{20}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{20}\n$$\n\nof the apples from basket $C$ are red."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4149b2fe-4243-595e-af7f-851b308f7eb1" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a415e615-de9f-575f-8327-5cb13875f6dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089514} {:answer/id #uuid "c29e02c7-5996-5ead-9fcb-d1399b421b50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089515} {:answer/id #uuid "cc4eda14-bbfd-5638-b2d4-19a8b1c5bed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089516} {:answer/id #uuid "04feab5e-8c7f-51a9-868c-f43b9fdefc66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089517} {:answer/id #uuid "0e465e1f-4ac3-5d7a-b706-2b79335c2745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089518}] :answer-field/id #uuid "06afc531-fa91-5019-b4e2-94c44b5a037f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089513}] :question/id #uuid "b28d6f09-2482-5acc-8192-10bb24fa4db6" :question/math-academy-id "q-143528" :question/problem "Alice has three email accounts.\nIn the first account, she received $16$ emails, of which $5$ were spam.\nIn the second account, she received $10$ emails, of which $2$ were spam.\nAnd in the third account, she received $14$ emails, of which $3$ were spam.\nBased on this data, what is the probability that a randomly selected email across all her accounts is spam?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092563 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0ebe700-63af-57d4-ae8f-9e09dac5cf76" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Conditional Probability Using the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d894d51-35fe-5b4d-ab7a-7e6c5458946b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089550} {:answer/id #uuid "a994961e-b077-521e-a843-cd021a01f391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089551} {:answer/id #uuid "adc6389e-4e2d-5b45-b1fc-cf755e9903a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "47%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089552} {:answer/id #uuid "52b17e54-1691-5516-9e05-438a180b1e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "55%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089553} {:answer/id #uuid "7a0e2926-d3c2-5a5a-be6e-0d8ae3e2a563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089554}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2a433e8e-3dc6-5341-b3ca-21d14fb92585" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089549}] :question/id #uuid "acc861ca-b4d8-56d2-9205-2d826d491014" :question/math-academy-id "q-144038" :question/problem "At a gas station, $40%$ of the customers buy Regular gasoline, $45%$ buy Premium gasoline, and $15%$ buy Supreme gasoline. It is known that $65%$ of the customers who purchase Regular gasoline and $50%$ of the customers who buy Premium gasoline pay with a credit card. If the probability that a randomly selected customer pays with a credit card is $57.5%$, what percentage of Supreme gasoline customers pay using a credit card?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
